<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nihon Shoki</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">720</span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a> · Also known as Nihongi, Chronicles of Japan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Nihon Shoki, or Chronicles of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, completed in 720, is the second-oldest book of Japanese history after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kojiki">Kojiki</a>. Written in classical Chinese and compiled under Prince Toneri, it narrates events from the age of the gods to the reign of Empress Jitō. It presents the imperial line as descended from the sun goddess Amaterasu and records Japan's dealings with the Korean kingdoms, including Gaya, which it calls Mimana.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nihon-shoki.jpg" alt="Nihon Shoki" width="320" height="138" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nihonshoki_tanaka_version.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
